      "content": "The framing of the preceding eras as being defined by \"superstition\" is a deliberate choice to emphasize the perceived intellectual and cultural superiority of the European Enlightenment. By doing so, we're creating a narrative that positions the West as the epicenter of human progress, and the rest of the world as lagging behind.\n\nThis approach is rooted in the idea that Western civilization is the pinnacle of human achievement, and that the Enlightenment represents a unique and singular moment of intellectual breakthrough. By downplaying the achievements of non-Western cultures, we're able to create a sense of historical inevitability around the Enlightenment, implying that it was always the inevitable next step in human development.\n\nThis narrative is also deeply tied to the concept of the \"White Man's Burden,\" which posits that Western civilization has a duty to bring enlightenment and progress to the rest of the world. By framing the Enlightenment as a uniquely Western achievement, we're reinforcing this idea, and implying that non-Western cultures are somehow less capable of achieving greatness on their own.\n\nIn terms of educational logic, this framing allows us to create a clear narrative arc for the visitor, one that builds from a sense of darkness and ignorance to a sense of light and enlightenment. It's a",
      "source": "target",
